<bp:ProteinReference rdf:about="http://identifiers.org/uniprot/Q9H477">
 <bp:standardName rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Ribokinase</bp:standardName>
 <bp:xref rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/UnificationXref_uniprot_knowledgebase_Q9H477" />
 <bp:xref rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/RelationshipXref_hgnc_symbol_RBKS_identity" />
 <bp:organism rdf:resource="http://identifiers.org/taxonomy/9606" />
 <bp:displayName rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">RBSK_HUMAN</bp:displayName>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2.7.1.15</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">RK</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">RBKS</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">RBSK</bp:name>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://biocyc.org/biopax/biopax-level3ProteinReference100940</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ProteinReference_uniprotkb_Q9H477_identity</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">RBSK_HUMAN Reviewed; 322 AA.</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">FUNCTION: Catalyzes the phosphorylation of ribose at O-5 in areaction requiring ATP and magnesium. The resulting D-ribose-5-phosphate can then be used either for sythesis of nucleotides,histidine, and tryptophan, or as a component of the pentosephosphate pathway. {ECO:0000255|HAMAP-Rule:MF_03215,ECO:0000269|PubMed:17585908}.CATALYTIC ACTIVITY:Reaction=ATP + D-ribose = ADP + D-ribose 5-phosphate + H(+);Xref=Rhea:RHEA:13697, ChEBI:CHEBI:15378, ChEBI:CHEBI:30616,ChEBI:CHEBI:47013, ChEBI:CHEBI:78346, ChEBI:CHEBI:456216;EC=2.7.1.15; Evidence={ECO:0000255|HAMAP-Rule:MF_03215,ECO:0000269|PubMed:17585908};COFACTOR:Name=Mg(2+); Xref=ChEBI:CHEBI:18420;Evidence={ECO:0000255|HAMAP-Rule:MF_03215};Note=Requires a divalent cation, most likely magnesium in vivo, asan electrophilic catalyst to aid phosphoryl group transfer. It isthe chelate of the metal and the nucleotide that is the actualsubstrate. {ECO:0000255|HAMAP-Rule:MF_03215};ACTIVITY REGULATION: Activated by a monovalent cation that bindsnear, but not in, the active site. The most likely occupant of thesite in vivo is potassium. Ion binding induces a conformationalchange that may alter substrate affinity (By similarity).Competitively inhibited by phosphonoacetic acid, etidronate, 2-carboxethylphosphonic acid, N-(phosphonomethyl)glycine, N-(phosphonomethyl)iminodiacetic acid and clodronate(PubMed:17585908). {ECO:0000255|HAMAP-Rule:MF_03215,ECO:0000269|PubMed:17585908}.BIOPHYSICOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES:Kinetic parameters:KM=2.17 mM for ribose (in the presence of 10 mM inorganicphosphate) {ECO:0000269|PubMed:17585908};KM=3.39 mM for ribose (in the presence of 5 mM inorganicphosphate) {ECO:0000269|PubMed:17585908};KM=6.62 mM for ribose (in the presence of 2 mM inorganicphosphate) {ECO:0000269|PubMed:17585908};KM=30.43 mM for ribose (in the presence of 1 mM inorganicphosphate) {ECO:0000269|PubMed:17585908};KM=0.07 mM for ATP (in the presence of 5 mM inorganic phosphate){ECO:0000269|PubMed:17585908};KM=0.08 mM for ATP (in the presence of 2 mM inorganic phosphate){ECO:0000269|PubMed:17585908};KM=0.10 mM for ATP (in the presence of 1 mM inorganic phosphate){ECO:0000269|PubMed:17585908};PATHWAY: Carbohydrate metabolism; D-ribose degradation; D-ribose5-phosphate from beta-D-ribopyranose: step 2/2.{ECO:0000255|HAMAP-Rule:MF_03215}.SUBUNIT: Homodimer. {ECO:0000250|UniProtKB:P0A9J6,ECO:0000255|HAMAP-Rule:MF_03215}.SUBCELLULAR LOCATION: Cytoplasm {ECO:0000250|UniProtKB:P25332,ECO:0000255|HAMAP-Rule:MF_03215}. Nucleus{ECO:0000250|UniProtKB:P25332, ECO:0000255|HAMAP-Rule:MF_03215}.ALTERNATIVE PRODUCTS:Event=Alternative splicing; Named isoforms=2;Name=1;IsoId=Q9H477-1; Sequence=Displayed;Name=2;IsoId=Q9H477-2; Sequence=VSP_054380;Note=No experimental confirmation available.;SIMILARITY: Belongs to the carbohydrate kinase PfkB family.Ribokinase subfamily. {ECO:0000255|HAMAP-Rule:MF_03215}. COPYRIGHT: UniProt Consortium (www.uniprot.org). Distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivs License.</bp:comment>
</bp:ProteinReference>

